\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The greatest operator supported on a retained subspace}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Under the hypotheses and notation of \ref{fdip-00K7}, the
\vocabk{shorted operator}{fdip-00K6} exists and is
##{T_0=\iota_XSp_X:V\to V.}
Thus #{T_0} is the greatest self-adjoint operator satisfying
#{0\leq T_0\leq A} and #{\im T_0\subseteq X}.
It is unique.}
\p{If the eliminated compression #{c:U\to U} is invertible,
then #{N=0}, #{W=U}, and the retained map is the
\vocabk{split Schur map}{fdip-001T}
##{S=a-bc^{-1}b^\dagger.}
If #{A>0}, then #{c>0} and #{S>0}; both are invertible.
Invertibility of #{c} alone does not imply #{S>0}.}

\proof{
\p{The projection-adjoint identities give
#{T_0^\dagger=\iota_XS^\dagger p_X=T_0}, and its range is contained
in #{X}. On #{v=\iota_Xx+\iota_Uu}, its quadratic value is
#{h(v,T_0v)=h_X(x,Sx)\geq0}. Square completion in
\ref{fdip-00K7} shows that #{q_A(v)-h(v,T_0v)\geq0}.
Hence #{T_0\in\mathcal C_X(A)}.}
\p{Let #{T\in\mathcal C_X(A)}. For #{u\in U} and #{v\in V},
self-adjointness and #{Tv\in X} give
##{h(T\iota_Uu,v)=h(\iota_Uu,Tv)=0.}
Nondegeneracy of #{h} implies #{T\iota_Uu=0}. Consequently the
quadratic value of #{T} on #{\iota_Xx+\iota_Uu} is its value on #{\iota_Xx},
independently of #{u}. Apply #{T\leq A} at the minimizing extension
#{u_*=-jc_W^{-1}d_Wx}:
##{\begin{aligned}
h(\iota_Xx,T\iota_Xx)
 &=h(\iota_Xx+\iota_Uu_*,T(\iota_Xx+\iota_Uu_*))\\
 &\leq q_A(\iota_Xx+\iota_Uu_*)=h_X(x,Sx).
\end{aligned}}
This is #{T\leq T_0} on every split vector, so #{T_0} is greatest.
Antisymmetry from \ref{fdip-00K3} proves uniqueness.}
\p{If #{c} is invertible, its kernel is zero and the canonical
identification #{W=U} turns the construction into
#{S=a-bc^{-1}d=a-bc^{-1}b^\dagger}.
If #{A>0}, then for nonzero #{u},
#{h_U(u,cu)=q_A(\iota_Uu)>0}, so #{c>0} and finite dimension makes
#{c} invertible. For nonzero #{x}, the minimizing vector
#{\iota_Xx-\iota_Uc^{-1}b^\dagger x} is nonzero, because its retained
projection is #{x}. Its strictly positive energy is #{h_X(x,Sx)};
hence #{S>0}, and finite dimension again gives invertibility.
The example in \ref{fdip-00K9} shows why the hypothesis #{A>0}
cannot be replaced by invertibility of #{c}.}
\p{For #{U=0}, the result gives #{T_0=A}; for #{X=0}, it gives
#{T_0=0}. If #{c=0}, it gives #{T_0=\iota_Xap_X}; if #{A=0}, it gives
#{T_0=0}. On #{V=0}, all maps and all forms are the unique zero-space
ones, so the same assertions apply. The range condition remains
containment in #{X}, even when #{S} has a kernel.}
}

\p{This greatestness statement is a deduction from
\ref{fdip-00K7} and the Loewner comparison; it does not require
an orthonormal basis. The real strict Schur equivalence, including
its converse, is proved separately in \ref{fdip-001Y}.
The real matrix minimum in
\citet{Appendix A.5.5, pp. 650-651}{boyd2004convex}
is consistent with this specialization; the ordered-field
greatestness argument is the one given above.}
